  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how different antibiotics interact with the cell envelope of a Gram-negative bacterium and why some drugs fail to reach their target. It shows the outer membrane, a peptidoglycan layer, and the inner membrane, emphasizing that the bacterial envelope is a major barrier.\n\nOne antibiotic appears to enter through a membrane pore, while another is shown staying outside the cell, suggesting poor penetration across the outer membrane. A third antibiotic is depicted deeper inside the envelope, near a target labeled PBP, indicating that successful treatment depends on getting through the membranes and reaching the correct intracellular or periplasmic site.\n\nAt the same time, an efflux pump spans the envelope and actively expels small drug molecules from inside the cell back out across the outer membrane. The arrows highlight this outward transport and suggest that even compounds that enter can be removed before accumulating to effective levels.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that antibiotic effectiveness in Gram-negative bacteria depends on a balance between uptake through pores, passage across the envelope, binding to targets such as PBP, and active extrusion by efflux pumps.",
